@page "/about"

<PageTitle>About Clarity Belongs</PageTitle>
<HeadContent>
    <meta name="description" content="Clarity Belongs helps people keep track of public websites, endpoints, domains, certificates, DNS, and service status without constant manual checking." />
</HeadContent>

<div class="shell public-shell article-shell">
    <header class="hero compact-hero article-hero">
        <div class="eyebrow">ABOUT CLARITY BELONGS</div>
        <h1>Clarity belongs to everyone.</h1>
        <p class="public-lead">Technology should help people keep track of important public information without demanding constant attention.</p>
    </header>

    <article class="article-body">
        <section>
            <h2>What Clarity does</h2>
            <p>Clarity Belongs checks supported public websites, endpoints, domains, certificates, DNS records, and service-status sources over time. It saves observations and change history so you can come back and review what happened.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>What Clarity does not do</h2>
            <p>Clarity does not monitor private networks, bypass access controls, or promise to understand every change automatically. It is designed for calm, factual, self-service monitoring of supported public targets.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>The ecosystem</h2>
            <div class="ecosystem-grid">
                <a href="https://softwarebelongs.com"><strong>Software Belongs</strong><span>DO — useful software for getting things done.</span></a>
                <div class="ecosystem-current"><strong>Clarity Belongs</strong><span>KNOW — keep an eye on what matters.</span></div>
                <div><strong>AutoPilot IT</strong><span>HANDLE — self-service technology for businesses.</span></div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>How we approach the product</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>Keep the experience self-service and understandable.</li>
                <li>Show factual status and saved evidence.</li>
                <li>Use calm language instead of alarmist language.</li>
                <li>Be clear about what each watch actually checks.</li>
                <li>Keep account history available so changes can be reviewed later.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </article>
</div>
